<HelpLayout title="Routines" description="How to create routines, plan their sets, start a workout from a routine, and share routines in Badger.">
  <h1>Routines</h1>

  <p>A routine is a saved training program: a set of days, each with its exercises and planned sets. Start a workout from a routine and Badger fills in the exercises and starting weights for you.</p>
  <p>Want a ready-made program instead? Badger includes ten; see <a href="#programs">Built-in programs</a>.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="creating">Create a routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>More → Routines</strong>, tap <strong>+</strong> and enter a name.</li>
    <li>Tap the routine to open it, then tap <strong>Add day</strong>.</li>
    <li>Tap the day, then tap <strong>Add exercise</strong> for each movement.</li>
    <li>Tap an exercise row to <a href="#predefined-sets">plan its sets</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>To turn workouts you've already logged into a routine, see <a href="#create-from-history">Create a routine from your history</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="starting">Start a workout from a routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Make the routine active: in the routine list, long-press it and tap <strong>Set active</strong>. Its days appear on the Today screen.</li>
    <li>On the Today screen, tap a day. The <strong>routine log preview</strong> opens with the day's exercises and their sets.</li>
    <li>Uncheck any exercise you want to leave out, or tap <strong>Swap</strong> to replace one for this workout only.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Start</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The preview also shows an estimated duration. During a <a href="/help/tools/#deload">deload week</a>, a deload bar at the top shows the reductions, with a toggle to use full weights for this workout. An exercise with a progression rule shows the weight its rule proposes; see <a href="/help/progression/#when-a-rule-acts">What the routine log preview shows</a>.</p>

  <h3>Which day comes next</h3>
  <p>Only one routine is active at a time, and it sits at the top of the routine list with an accent border. On an alternating program (A/B days not tied to weekdays), the Today screen highlights the next day in the sequence and moves it to the top once you finish the one before.</p>
  <p>You can still start any day. If the days are assigned to weekdays, the Today screen highlights today's.</p>

  <h3 id="weight-source">Where starting weights come from (weight source)</h3>
  <p>A segmented control on the routine detail screen sets this:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Last perf.</strong> (default) - The weight from your most recent workout of each exercise, wherever that workout came from.</li>
    <li><strong>Routine</strong> - The planned set weights only. A set marked "Copy from previous" uses its planned value.</li>
    <li><strong>Both</strong> - Planned weights first, then your last performance where none is set.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="history-scope">Which past workouts count (history scope)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Any workout</strong> (default) - Looks at all your previous workouts.</li>
    <li><strong>This routine</strong> - Only workouts started from this routine. Use this when you run two programs at once.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="rep-target">Where reps come from (rep target)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Last perf.</strong> (default) - Reps copy from your last session.</li>
    <li><strong>Plan</strong> - Reps stay pinned to the planned sets. Use this for strict programs such as StrongLifts 5×5, where the rep count shouldn't drift.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Weight still follows the weight source. An exercise with a <a href="/help/progression/#rules">progression rule</a> always takes its reps from the plan.</p>

  <h2 id="predefined-sets">Plan the sets</h2>
  <p>Tap an exercise row in a routine day to open its planned sets. Drag to reorder them. Each set can have:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weight</strong> - A value, or "Copy from previous" to use the weight from your most recent session.</li>
    <li><strong>Reps</strong> - A value, or "Copy from previous".</li>
    <li><strong>Rep range</strong> - A top end, so the target reads "8-10". See <a href="#rep-ranges">Rep ranges</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Open target (AMRAP)</strong> - No top end, so the target reads "8+". See <a href="#amrap-targets">Open-topped targets</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Warmup flag</strong> - Marks the set as a warmup.</li>
    <li><strong>RPE or RIR</strong> - How hard the set should feel. See <a href="#intensity-targets">Intensity targets</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Percentage</strong>, <strong>driver</strong> and <strong>week</strong> - Progression controls, shown when the exercise has a rule or the day is waved. See <a href="/help/progression/">Progression</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On a timed exercise, type a duration as a time or in seconds: <strong>15:00</strong> or <strong>900</strong> for a fifteen-minute ride. See <a href="/help/logging/#duration-entry">Typing a duration</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="rep-ranges">Rep ranges</h3>
  <p>Tap <strong>+ rep range</strong> under a working set and set the top end. The target then reads "8-10" in the editor, on the routine log preview, above the set recorder while you train, and on the workout summary.</p>
  <p>The lower number is the one to hit: 8 reps or more on an "8-10" set meets the plan. Below 8 is under plan, marked on the summary with a small caret. Warmups can't have a range.</p>
  <p>A range is what the <a href="/help/progression/#double-progression">double progression</a> suggestion works from.</p>

  <h3 id="amrap-targets">Open-topped targets (AMRAP)</h3>
  <p>Tap <strong>+ AMRAP</strong> under a working set and the target reads <strong>8+</strong>: at least 8 reps, then as many as you can that day. <strong>AMRAP</strong> stands for <em>as many reps as possible</em>, and <strong>1+</strong> is how you program a set to failure.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A set is either ranged or open: setting one replaces the other. Warmups can't be open either.</li>
    <li>An 8+ set meets the plan at 8.</li>
    <li>Raise the rep number and the set stays open: an 8+ becomes a 12+.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See <a href="/help/logging/#amrap">AMRAP and to failure</a> for logging one.</p>

  <h3 id="intensity-targets">Intensity targets (RPE and RIR)</h3>
  <p>A set can say how hard it should feel as well as how heavy: <strong>RPE 8</strong> or <strong>RIR 2</strong>. Tap <strong>+ RPE</strong> or <strong>+ RIR</strong> beside the rep-target controls on a working set, and use the same stepper you <a href="/help/logging/#rpe-rir">log with</a>.</p>
  <p>The target shows beside the set on the log preview, and above the entry area on the training screen: <em>Plan 8-10 @ RPE 8</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Pick one: setting RPE clears RIR, and the other way round.</li>
    <li>Targets are whole numbers, matching how you log RPE. A half-step target in an imported file rounds up.</li>
    <li>Warmups can't have a target.</li>
    <li>Badger shows the target beside what you log and doesn't compare the two.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Quick edit</h3>
  <p><strong>Quick edit</strong>, next to <strong>Day options</strong> on each day card, puts every exercise on the day on one screen, with fields for its first working set: weight and reps, or whichever fields the exercise type uses. Change a value and submit to save. Tap an exercise name to open its full set editor.</p>
  <p>Quick edit shows a rep range read-only. Raise the reps past the top of the range and the range is dropped for an exact target.</p>

  <h3 id="per-exercise-overrides">Per-exercise settings in a routine</h3>
  <p>Each exercise in a routine also has settings of its own, separate from the planned sets:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Weight increment override</li>
    <li>Notes override</li>
    <li>Tempo</li>
    <li>Default machine and attachment</li>
    <li>Default bar</li>
    <li>Default grip type (when the machine has grip types)</li>
    <li id="default-accessories">Default accessories - the band, chain or straps you always use on that exercise, attached for you each time you log the day. Sets with an accessory attached don't count toward <a href="/help/progress/#prs">personal records</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Set rest time on the exercise itself (Exercise → settings), and it applies everywhere you train it. The routine's <strong>Rest (seconds)</strong> field is used only when the exercise has no rest of its own.</p>

  <h2>Organize days and routines</h2>
  <h3>Schedule a day</h3>
  <p>Open the day's <strong>Day options</strong> and set <strong>Scheduled days</strong> to pick weekdays. A <strong>calendar icon</strong> then appears on the day row - tap it to change them. The day shows as scheduled on the Today screen on those days, and as short labels (Mon, Wed, Fri) on the row. A warning appears if two days share a weekday.</p>

  <h3>Day options</h3>
  <p>Open a day's <strong>Day options</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Default gym</strong> - The gym this day is done at.</li>
    <li><strong>Notes</strong> - Shown as a banner at the top of the training screen when you start the day, such as "Focus on pause reps today". Dismissing it hides it for that workout only.</li>
    <li><strong>Wave</strong> - Run the day as a cycle of weeks. See <a href="/help/progression/#waves">Waved days</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="routine-notes">Routine notes</h3>
  <p>The <strong>Notes</strong> section on the routine detail screen holds notes for the whole program: why you chose it, the current cycle, a target date. Tap it (or <strong>Add notes</strong>) to open the editor. Routine notes go with the routine when you share it as a <code>.badger</code> file.</p>

  <h3>Folders</h3>
  <p>Tap the folder icon in the top bar of the routine list to create a folder. Long-press a routine to move it into one. Routines outside any folder appear under "Other".</p>

  <h2 id="progression">Progression</h2>
  <p>Progression rules, training maxes, percentage sets, waves and the overload suggestion are on the <a href="/help/progression/">Progression</a> page:</p>
  <ul>
    <li id="writing-a-rule"><a href="/help/progression/#writing-a-rule">Write a progression rule</a></li>
    <li id="set-up-progression"><a href="/help/progression/#set-up-progression">Add rules to a routine you already have</a></li>
    <li id="reading-a-rule"><a href="/help/progression/#reading-a-rule">Where a rule shows</a></li>
    <li id="when-a-rule-acts"><a href="/help/progression/#when-a-rule-acts">What the routine log preview shows</a></li>
    <li id="training-max"><a href="/help/progression/#training-max">Training max</a></li>
    <li id="percent-sets"><a href="/help/progression/#percent-sets">Percentage sets</a></li>
    <li id="percent-of-another-lift"><a href="/help/progression/#percent-of-another-lift">A percentage of another lift</a></li>
    <li id="driver-set"><a href="/help/progression/#driver-set">The driver set</a></li>
    <li id="waves"><a href="/help/progression/#waves">Waved days</a></li>
    <li id="rotating-exercises"><a href="/help/progression/#rotating-exercises">Rotate exercises by week</a></li>
    <li id="double-progression"><a href="/help/progression/#double-progression">Double progression</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Supersets in routines</h2>
  <p>Open the <strong>⋮ menu</strong> on an exercise in the routine detail screen, choose <strong>Superset…</strong>, check the exercises to group and confirm. Tap the group's colored header to rename, recolor, reorder, add or remove exercises, or dissolve it. Workouts started from the routine keep the groups. See <a href="/help/logging/#supersets">Supersets</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="alternatives">Alternative exercises</h2>
  <p>Give an exercise up to two alternatives so you can swap mid-workout when a machine is taken or something hurts.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>On the routine detail screen, open the exercise's <strong>⋮ menu</strong> and choose <strong>Manage alternatives…</strong>.</li>
    <li>Search for and add up to two alternatives. Changes save immediately.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Each alternative row's own <strong>⋮ menu</strong> can remove it (<strong>Remove alternative</strong>) or make it the default.</p>
  <p>To swap during the workout, open the training screen's <strong>⋮ menu</strong> and tap <strong>Switch to alternative…</strong>. The original is listed too, labeled <strong>(original)</strong>, so you can switch back. You can also pick an alternative before you start, from the swap icon on the routine log preview.</p>
  <p>A swap lasts for that workout only. The new exercise comes in with its own recent weights, your routine stays the same, and your history records what you actually did.</p>

  <h3 id="plan-b">Plan B: when there's no alternative set up</h3>
  <p>Open the training screen's <strong>⋮ menu</strong> and tap <strong>Plan B - swap exercise…</strong> (it reads <strong>Switch to alternative…</strong> when alternatives exist, and opens the same sheet). This works in free workouts too.</p>
  <p>The <strong>Plan B</strong> section lists up to eight substitutes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>They share a <strong>primary muscle</strong> with the exercise you're replacing.</li>
    <li>They're <strong>available where you are</strong>. A machine exercise appears only when a machine linked to it is at your workout's gym. Barbell, dumbbell, cable, band, kettlebell and bodyweight exercises always appear, and so does every machine exercise when the workout has no gym.</li>
    <li>Exercises you've marked <strong>avoided</strong> are left out, as are the other exercises in the same superset.</li>
    <li>Exercises <strong>you've done</strong> come first, most recent first, with a "last done" date. Matches on equipment type sort ahead of the rest.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Nothing fits? Tap <strong>Browse library</strong> at the bottom to open the full exercise picker. Every swap works the same way: this workout only, with the substitute's own recent weights.</p>

  <h4>If you've already completed sets</h4>
  <p>Badger asks first. By default, the completed sets stay on the exercise you did, and the substitute takes the remaining sets. If you logged under the wrong exercise, check the box to move everything to the new one. The Swap button's text changes to match.</p>

  <h4>Make an alternative the permanent default</h4>
  <p>For a longer stretch (an injury, travel, a machine that's gone), open the alternative's <strong>⋮ menu</strong> on the Manage alternatives screen and tap <strong>Make default</strong>. It becomes the routine's exercise, and the old one moves into the alternatives list so you can switch back later.</p>
  <p>The confirmation offers to set the new exercise's target weights from its own history. Its machine, attachment and grip defaults reset; the set and rep scheme stays.</p>

  <h2>Duplicate a routine</h2>
  <p>Long-press a routine, tap <strong>Duplicate</strong> and name the copy. It includes all days, exercises, planned sets and groups.</p>

  <h2 id="create-from-history">Create a routine from your history</h2>
  <p>Turn past workouts into a multi-day routine, one day per workout, with their exercises, sets and warmups.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>History</strong> and switch to <strong>List</strong> view.</li>
    <li>Long-press a workout card, then tap more cards to select them.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Create routine</strong> in the top bar.</li>
    <li>Follow the four steps:
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Name</strong> - Name the routine.</li>
        <li><strong>Settings</strong> - Choose a weight source and history scope.</li>
        <li><strong>Day names</strong> - Each day is named after its workout's weekday ("Monday"). Rename any of them.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirm</strong> - Review and tap <strong>Create routine</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>The new routine opens right away, with days ordered oldest to newest. To cancel, tap <strong>✕</strong> or press back.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="sharing">Share a routine</h2>
  <p>Two options sit side by side in the routine's <strong>⋮</strong> menu:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Send routine file</strong> shares a <code>.badger</code> file. Whoever opens it gets the routine itself, ready to use.</li>
    <li><strong>Share as image</strong> makes a card of the plan in your theme and accent, sized for a post. You see a preview, then your phone's share sheet opens.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>From the routine's <strong>⋮</strong> menu, the image is an <strong>overview</strong>: the routine's name, days per week, and each day's exercises, sets and reps. From a day's <strong>⋮</strong> menu, it's that <strong>day</strong>: the program and day name, a "Day 1 of 4" mark, a summary line, and each exercise's set count and target.</p>
  <p>On a day card, a lift with a progression rule has the rule printed under it. If every lift on the day has the same rule, it's printed once at the bottom. A lift whose sets change by week or stage (5/3/1's main lift, a GZCLP tier) shows all of them as a short ladder with no current mark.</p>

  <h3 id="sharing-weights">Weights on a shared image</h3>
  <p>The image shows sets and reps without weights unless you turn on <strong>Include my weights</strong> on the share sheet. The switch starts off each time. Percentage sets always show their percentage (say 65% of your training max), with the switch on or off.</p>

  <h3 id="sharing-rules">What a routine file carries</h3>
  <p>A <code>.badger</code> file carries the days, sets, rep targets, routine notes, progression rules, percentage sets, driver flags and waves. It doesn't carry your training max, photos or private setup notes, so whoever opens it sets their own training max. See <a href="/badger-format/#progression">the format reference</a>.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="programs">Built-in programs</h2>
  <p>Badger includes 10 programs in 3 folders, on a <strong>new install only</strong>. If you've used Badger a while, download the one you want from the <a href="/programs/">Programs</a> page, or add rules to a routine you already have with <a href="/help/progression/#set-up-progression">Set up progression</a>. Six of the programs come with <a href="/help/progression/#rules">progression rules</a> already written.</p>

  <h3>Beginner</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>StrongLifts 5×5</strong> - Two alternating full-body days (A and B), 5 sets of 5 on the major compound lifts. Adds weight every session you complete, drops 10% after three misses in a row, and moves down a <a href="/help/progression/#stages">stage ladder</a> (5×5, 3×5, 1×5) as you stall.</li>
    <li><strong>Starting Strength</strong> - Similar to StrongLifts, with slightly different exercises and 3×5 progression. When you stall, its rule halves your increment instead of deloading you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Intermediate</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>GZCLP</strong> - Three tiers: heavy compounds (T1), medium compounds (T2) and accessory work (T3). Each tier has its own rule; T1 and T2 also have their own <a href="/help/progression/#stages">stage ladder</a>, but T3's rule has none.</li>
    <li><strong>5/3/1 BBB</strong> - Wendler's 5/3/1 main sets, then Boring But Big 5×10, both as percentages of one <a href="/help/progression/#training-max">training max</a> on a four-week <a href="/help/progression/#waves">wave</a>. Set the training max once per lift; the rule raises it at the end of each cycle unless the plus set came up short.</li>
    <li><strong>nSuns 531LP</strong> - A long percentage ramp each session off a training max, with one <a href="/help/progression/#driver-set">driver set</a> whose reps decide the week's increment.</li>
    <li><strong>Madcow 5×5</strong> - Weekly progression. Every set is a percentage of the lift's top set, and all three days of a lift <a href="/help/progression/#progressing-together">share that number</a>. It deloads 10% after two missed weeks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Splits</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>PPL + Upper/Lower 5-Day</strong> - Push, pull and legs, then an upper and a lower day, with rep ranges instead of fixed rep counts.</li>
    <li><strong>PPL 6-Day</strong> - Push/Pull/Legs twice per week.</li>
    <li><strong>Upper/Lower 4-Day</strong> - Two upper-body and two lower-body days per week.</li>
    <li><strong>Full Body 3-Day</strong> - Three full-body sessions per week.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Note:</strong> Built-in programs use standard exercise names. If you've renamed or customized exercises, you may need to swap them in the routine after installing.
  </div>
</HelpLayout>
